## HLA expression in edited cells
Our editing strategy produces HLA-E single-chain genes that are expressed from the B2M promoter, so they should be regulated appro­ priately in response to cytokines. Undifferentiated ESCs with the B2M genotypes shown in Figure 1 were cultured with and without IFN- $\cdot\gamma,$ and the levels of cell surface HLA class I molecules were determined by flow cytometry (Fig. 2). In the absence of IFN- $\cdot\gamma,$ $B2M^{+/+}$ ESCs expressed nearly undetectable amounts of both HLA-E and polymor­ phic class I molecules (HLA-A, B and C), all of which were upregu­ lated significantly in the presence of IFN- $\cdot\gamma.$ In contrast, $B2M^{-/-}$ ESCs had no surface expression of HLA class I molecules, even in the pres­ ence of IFN- $\L\cdot\gamma,$ confirming that these cells have a complete knockout of B2M (although they acquired an abnormal tetraploid karyotype).
